00:03:00.190 --> 00:03:03.710
So regardless of the sector
an enterprise organization

00:03:03.710 --> 00:03:08.330
is operating within, there are
quite a lot of common themes

00:03:08.330 --> 00:03:10.710
that we all strive
to achieve to,

00:03:10.710 --> 00:03:16.800
especially in the context
of a global digital market.

00:03:16.800 --> 00:03:19.170
We see these things
across pretty much all

00:03:19.170 --> 00:03:21.660
of our customers and
actually we recognize

00:03:21.660 --> 00:03:23.850
those exact same
things internally

00:03:23.850 --> 00:03:25.980
within our own organization.

00:03:25.980 --> 00:03:28.780
And they're
relatively simplistic.

00:03:28.780 --> 00:03:31.650
We all strive for
agility, the ability

00:03:31.650 --> 00:03:35.860
to be the disruptor
rather than the disrupted.

00:03:35.860 --> 00:03:38.310
We strive to have
data driven businesses

00:03:38.310 --> 00:03:41.820
where the decision making
is based on analytics

00:03:41.820 --> 00:03:44.250
and real time data.

00:03:44.250 --> 00:03:46.800
We want to increase our revenue
base through the introduction

00:03:46.800 --> 00:03:48.600
of differentiated offerings.

00:03:48.600 --> 00:03:52.673
And we want to do that in a way
where we are cost controlling

00:03:52.673 --> 00:03:54.840
and making sure the solutions
we're pulling together

00:03:54.840 --> 00:03:57.131
are cost optimal
and competitive.

00:04:00.080 --> 00:04:03.830
So to achieve these aims,
there is quite a lot of change

00:04:03.830 --> 00:04:05.080
that's required.

00:04:05.080 --> 00:04:06.830
Business processes
need to change in order

00:04:06.830 --> 00:04:08.420
to provide agility.

00:04:08.420 --> 00:04:10.370
Security processes
need to change in order

00:04:10.370 --> 00:04:11.330
to provide agility.

00:04:11.330 --> 00:04:13.970
And equally your application
development methodologies

00:04:13.970 --> 00:04:19.730
themselves need to be changing
to give you that agility.

00:04:19.730 --> 00:04:23.030
Looking at a data driven
decision making scenario,

00:04:23.030 --> 00:04:25.160
again, your
application space needs

00:04:25.160 --> 00:04:27.080
to be able to support that.

00:04:27.080 --> 00:04:29.210
There needs to be a
consolidation of your data

00:04:29.210 --> 00:04:31.580
in order to be able
to effectively apply

00:04:31.580 --> 00:04:35.600
machine learning and
analytics to your data.

00:04:35.600 --> 00:04:39.450
And equally from a revenue
and cost perspective,

00:04:39.450 --> 00:04:41.210
well, in order to
actually find what's

00:04:41.210 --> 00:04:43.790
a differentiator in the
market, you need real data.

00:04:43.790 --> 00:04:47.600
And you need the real
analytics against that data

00:04:47.600 --> 00:04:50.720
to understand what the
demand in the market is.

00:04:50.720 --> 00:04:52.850
Automation plays
absolutely huge part

00:04:52.850 --> 00:04:55.390
in ensuring that we got
a cost effective solution

00:04:55.390 --> 00:04:58.430
and we are able to control
the cost and be competitive.

00:04:58.430 --> 00:05:00.250
And equally, cloud
native solutions

00:05:00.250 --> 00:05:01.820
enable you to really--

00:05:01.820 --> 00:05:02.780
it's the utility model.

00:05:02.780 --> 00:05:05.690
It's utilizing resources
at the point you need them,

00:05:05.690 --> 00:05:08.780
being able to scale up and
down in line with agility

00:05:08.780 --> 00:05:11.370
of your business.

00:05:11.370 --> 00:05:14.360
So I want to drill down another
level further down from this

00:05:14.360 --> 00:05:17.270
and look at really what are the
transformational spaces that we

00:05:17.270 --> 00:05:18.120
need to look into.

00:05:22.795 --> 00:05:24.420
So from a business
process perspective,

00:05:24.420 --> 00:05:28.192
this is the absolute
bare minimum.

00:05:28.192 --> 00:05:30.650
We need to enable the business
to be able to make decisions

00:05:30.650 --> 00:05:33.550
based on data and analytics.

00:05:33.550 --> 00:05:36.050
IT operations.

00:05:36.050 --> 00:05:40.120
Majority of our organizations we
work with are still ITIL based.

00:05:40.120 --> 00:05:42.410
They use IT service
management tooling

00:05:42.410 --> 00:05:46.020
in order to manage the
environments and processes.

00:05:46.020 --> 00:05:49.740
And to achieve the actual
agility that we require,

00:05:49.740 --> 00:05:51.690
we need to start moving
more and more and more

00:05:51.690 --> 00:05:52.620
towards DevSecops.

00:05:52.620 --> 00:05:54.960
You're going to hear me
repeat the word DevSecOps over

00:05:54.960 --> 00:05:55.810
and over in this.

00:05:55.810 --> 00:05:57.970
And hopefully it's nothing
new to anyone here.

00:05:57.970 --> 00:05:59.970
But it's actually really
complex and really hard

00:05:59.970 --> 00:06:02.730
space to get into, especially
when your starting point

00:06:02.730 --> 00:06:07.210
is traditional IT operations.

00:06:07.210 --> 00:06:08.230
Security.

00:06:08.230 --> 00:06:12.100
Again, embedding security in the
application development space

00:06:12.100 --> 00:06:16.817
as opposed to it being a
wrapper after the fact.

00:06:16.817 --> 00:06:18.400
And the bigger
challenge at the moment

00:06:18.400 --> 00:06:23.920
is we're in the era of
multi-cloud, hybrid cloud,

00:06:23.920 --> 00:06:25.690
on-prem solutions.

00:06:25.690 --> 00:06:28.180
And coming up with
processes and technologies

00:06:28.180 --> 00:06:31.600
and tools actually are able
to effectively span all three

00:06:31.600 --> 00:06:33.640
of those spaces to give
you a consolidated view

00:06:33.640 --> 00:06:35.860
of your security
is a tough task,

00:06:35.860 --> 00:06:38.112
similar with your
processes around security.

00:06:40.830 --> 00:06:42.870
Not unlike the rest
of IT services,

00:06:42.870 --> 00:06:48.340
I think automation is absolutely
key within the security space.

00:06:48.340 --> 00:06:51.150
So we need to look at
the ways of automating

00:06:51.150 --> 00:06:55.890
security processes, security
testing, security governance,

00:06:55.890 --> 00:07:00.720
within our, essentially, things
like our CI/CD pipelines,

00:07:00.720 --> 00:07:04.020
policy driven architectures
whereby we can articulate

00:07:04.020 --> 00:07:05.940
policies-- that's
security policies

00:07:05.940 --> 00:07:09.810
and other business policies-- in
a way where the technology can

00:07:09.810 --> 00:07:10.410
enforce them.

00:07:13.080 --> 00:07:18.360
And anyone who is working
in regulated industries

00:07:18.360 --> 00:07:20.010
or with governmental
bodies will be

00:07:20.010 --> 00:07:23.400
aware of the amount
of work that needs

00:07:23.400 --> 00:07:25.350
to be done with the
legislative bodies,

00:07:25.350 --> 00:07:28.800
with the government bodies,
in essentially trying

00:07:28.800 --> 00:07:32.970
to bring him up to speed with
the cloud native technologies.

00:07:32.970 --> 00:07:35.130
They have to go on the
same transformation,

00:07:35.130 --> 00:07:38.060
and understanding, and
learning that we all do.

00:07:38.060 --> 00:07:39.810
And we have to bring
them along with us.

00:07:39.810 --> 00:07:42.150
So this is not a short term fix.

00:07:42.150 --> 00:07:43.840
And it's going to
take some time.

00:07:43.840 --> 00:07:45.990
And later on we'll
look at some ways

00:07:45.990 --> 00:07:48.420
that we can try to help
accelerate at least parts

00:07:48.420 --> 00:07:54.000
of this process so that the
whole thing is not [INAUDIBLE]..

00:07:54.000 --> 00:07:55.200
Application development.

00:07:55.200 --> 00:07:58.890
Back to DevSecOps, putting the
appropriate practices in place

00:07:58.890 --> 00:08:01.290
so we are able to
effectively get agility out

00:08:01.290 --> 00:08:04.890
of our application
development space,

00:08:04.890 --> 00:08:08.430
putting the right quality
assurance and testing

00:08:08.430 --> 00:08:11.160
capabilities, including the
security we touched upon within

00:08:11.160 --> 00:08:15.460
our CI/CD pipelines, adoption
of all these new technologies--

00:08:15.460 --> 00:08:19.260
so Kubernetes cloud
functions, X as a service--

00:08:19.260 --> 00:08:22.920
by X as a service I'm talking
about things like PaaS,

00:08:22.920 --> 00:08:25.950
SaaS, database as a
service, AML as a service,

00:08:25.950 --> 00:08:27.018
big data as a service.

00:08:27.018 --> 00:08:28.560
So all of those
types of technologies

00:08:28.560 --> 00:08:29.820
need to be adopted.

00:08:29.820 --> 00:08:31.350
Why do they need to be adopted?

00:08:31.350 --> 00:08:34.710
Because they form the building
blocks of your applications.

00:08:34.710 --> 00:08:36.299
Your applications
developers do not

00:08:36.299 --> 00:08:38.270
have to recreate those
things from scratch.

00:08:38.270 --> 00:08:40.308
They're ready to be consumed.

00:08:40.308 --> 00:08:42.600
And it's critical that we
start moving our applications

00:08:42.600 --> 00:08:45.480
towards using those ready made
services and building blocks

00:08:45.480 --> 00:08:49.230
that are managed by
managed service providers

00:08:49.230 --> 00:08:52.290
so your business can
concentrate on developing

00:08:52.290 --> 00:08:53.760
applications for
a line of business

00:08:53.760 --> 00:08:56.700
as opposed to caring
about under the hood IT.

00:08:59.427 --> 00:09:00.510
We've got to be realistic.

00:09:00.510 --> 00:09:03.150
At this point in time,
the vast majority

00:09:03.150 --> 00:09:05.850
of the applications that
are in the enterprise space

00:09:05.850 --> 00:09:08.070
are still not cloud native.

00:09:08.070 --> 00:09:12.720
And at a rough guess, I would
say 95% of the applications

00:09:12.720 --> 00:09:14.400
are not cloud native.

00:09:14.400 --> 00:09:17.490
And that's probably
somewhat generous.

00:09:17.490 --> 00:09:19.920
So we still got to
be aware that we've

00:09:19.920 --> 00:09:22.110
got a significant
portion of work

00:09:22.110 --> 00:09:26.460
to do in order to
transform, replace, refactor

00:09:26.460 --> 00:09:28.830
those applications to get
them to a cloud native space.

00:09:31.820 --> 00:09:33.320
Data consolidation.

00:09:33.320 --> 00:09:36.450
So separation of the code
from data is absolutely key.

00:09:36.450 --> 00:09:39.450
And especially when we
start looking at technology

00:09:39.450 --> 00:09:43.730
such as Kubernetes where,
essentially, the code

00:09:43.730 --> 00:09:48.030
base is running in a pod,
that pod can be killed.

00:09:48.030 --> 00:09:50.450
And it can be recreated
as many times as you like.

00:09:50.450 --> 00:09:51.320
It can be scaled up.

00:09:51.320 --> 00:09:52.760
It can be scaled down.

00:09:52.760 --> 00:09:54.410
The data mustn't be
affected from that.

00:09:54.410 --> 00:09:57.260
So separating code
from data is absolutely

00:09:57.260 --> 00:09:58.770
crucial at this stage.

00:09:58.770 --> 00:10:02.430
And it's a real difficult task
to do with legacy applications.

00:10:02.430 --> 00:10:04.220
But we also have to
look at centralization

00:10:04.220 --> 00:10:06.380
and normalization of
that data in order

00:10:06.380 --> 00:10:08.690
to then effectively
apply analytics.

00:10:12.580 --> 00:10:17.050
If anyone's worked with the
AI/ML type of technologies,

00:10:17.050 --> 00:10:21.310
machine learning, it's
somewhat of an area

00:10:21.310 --> 00:10:23.830
where there is a lot of
experimentation required.

00:10:23.830 --> 00:10:27.940
You're not always sure about the
viability of a particular idea

00:10:27.940 --> 00:10:32.010
being modelable in an effective
way through machine learning.

00:10:32.010 --> 00:10:35.070
So experimentation is required.

00:10:35.070 --> 00:10:36.460
And we need to
build capabilities

00:10:36.460 --> 00:10:38.250
where we can do
proof of concepts

00:10:38.250 --> 00:10:40.510
but very rapidly take a
proof of proof of concept

00:10:40.510 --> 00:10:43.900
that we've shown to be working
into a production level

00:10:43.900 --> 00:10:44.933
application.

00:10:48.910 --> 00:10:50.660
And this is probably
worthwhile mentioning

00:10:50.660 --> 00:10:54.650
from an AI/ML perspective
and analytics as a whole.

00:10:54.650 --> 00:10:56.450
It's a bi-directional
relationship

00:10:56.450 --> 00:10:58.310
in terms of the
way that it drives

00:10:58.310 --> 00:11:00.500
the business or the
business drives it.

00:11:00.500 --> 00:11:03.710
So your practices
from AI/ML perspective

00:11:03.710 --> 00:11:07.130
have got to be able to
respond to business demand

00:11:07.130 --> 00:11:09.950
and be able to model
things and predict things

00:11:09.950 --> 00:11:11.390
as the business requires.

00:11:11.390 --> 00:11:13.490
But equally we've got to
be open to the challenges

00:11:13.490 --> 00:11:18.770
that information
and analytics led

00:11:18.770 --> 00:11:21.740
sources can lead to us
changing our business models.

00:11:24.290 --> 00:11:27.230
Automation, again, is
an absolute key theme

00:11:27.230 --> 00:11:31.310
throughout this presentation in
being able to automate as much

00:11:31.310 --> 00:11:33.260
of the processes
because your agility is

00:11:33.260 --> 00:11:34.430
highly dependent on it.

00:11:34.430 --> 00:11:37.280
And we're getting into
a space at the moment

00:11:37.280 --> 00:11:42.570
where more and more things are
getting easier to automate.

00:11:42.570 --> 00:11:46.220
And we got to ensure things
like quality assurance, testing,

00:11:46.220 --> 00:11:49.580
and the security side are
really effectively addressed

00:11:49.580 --> 00:11:51.710
through the automation piece.

00:11:51.710 --> 00:11:54.120
And CI/CD is one answer.

00:11:54.120 --> 00:11:55.830
CI/CD is a methodology.

00:11:55.830 --> 00:11:59.150
CI/CD enables you to
encode your business rules

00:11:59.150 --> 00:12:03.590
and practices, your security
capabilities, testing.

00:12:03.590 --> 00:12:05.600
From our own
internal perspective,

00:12:05.600 --> 00:12:08.990
for example when we're
developing our cloud platforms,

00:12:08.990 --> 00:12:12.830
our CI/CD pipelines
actually include whole set

00:12:12.830 --> 00:12:17.180
of security testing around
scanning the code for quality,

00:12:17.180 --> 00:12:21.770
for inadvertent insertion
of vulnerabilities,

00:12:21.770 --> 00:12:25.910
for upstream reliance
on upstream libraries

00:12:25.910 --> 00:12:29.060
that may have vulnerabilities
included within them,

00:12:29.060 --> 00:12:30.930
and the QA side
of things equally.

00:12:30.930 --> 00:12:34.640
So we want to get to a point
where an application developer

00:12:34.640 --> 00:12:38.550
checking code in
automatically triggers

00:12:38.550 --> 00:12:41.700
a whole set of pipelines that
would automatically give you

00:12:41.700 --> 00:12:43.500
to a point where
you have confidence

00:12:43.500 --> 00:12:47.100
that code is sufficiently
tested to directly deploy

00:12:47.100 --> 00:12:47.910
into production.

00:12:47.910 --> 00:12:49.560
That's where you get
your agility from.

00:12:49.560 --> 00:12:52.560
And that's got to cover
security and the technology

00:12:52.560 --> 00:12:55.702
and everything
else to do with it.

00:12:55.702 --> 00:12:56.910
It's got to be policy driven.

00:12:56.910 --> 00:12:58.830
Again, going back to
the point of being

00:12:58.830 --> 00:13:03.220
able to encode business rules,
policies, legislative rules,

00:13:03.220 --> 00:13:06.240
policies, all of those things
in a way where they can

00:13:06.240 --> 00:13:10.650
programmatically be tested as
part of your ability to deploy

00:13:10.650 --> 00:13:15.240
and also to build
your solutions.

00:13:15.240 --> 00:13:17.580
And we've got to adopt all
the cloud native technology.

00:13:17.580 --> 00:13:23.280
So those who are familiar
with the Kubernetes space

00:13:23.280 --> 00:13:27.180
probably understand the
analogy of cattle versus pet.

00:13:27.180 --> 00:13:31.650
When we look at the current
existing set of applications

00:13:31.650 --> 00:13:33.480
we're dealing with,
enterprise applications

00:13:33.480 --> 00:13:36.120
are typically very stateful.

00:13:36.120 --> 00:13:38.550
We really care about the
state of our virtual machines

00:13:38.550 --> 00:13:39.480
and our servers.

00:13:39.480 --> 00:13:42.000
If they die, they take
things down with them

00:13:42.000 --> 00:13:46.050
that affects our business.

00:13:46.050 --> 00:13:49.840
In cloud native world, we are in
a complete different scenario.

00:13:49.840 --> 00:13:52.050
We don't patch a server.

00:13:52.050 --> 00:13:54.270
We create a new pod.

00:13:54.270 --> 00:13:55.530
We create a new pod.

00:13:55.530 --> 00:13:58.860
We start directing some of
the traffic towards that pod.

00:13:58.860 --> 00:14:01.300
If it's successful
and it's not failing,

00:14:01.300 --> 00:14:03.960
we start diverting all of
the traffic to the new pod

00:14:03.960 --> 00:14:06.090
and we get rid of the old one.

00:14:06.090 --> 00:14:10.140
Mentalitywise, it's
a mindset shift.

00:14:10.140 --> 00:14:13.530
Your application development
and QA processes is a shift

00:14:13.530 --> 00:14:16.820
and actually gaining agility.

00:14:16.820 --> 00:14:20.610
But it has a reliance or your
applications being architected

00:14:20.610 --> 00:14:24.140
in a way where they're able to
support that kind of mechanism.

00:14:24.140 --> 00:14:25.910
And your operational
practices has

00:14:25.910 --> 00:14:30.860
got to be aligned to being
able to do that kind of testing

00:14:30.860 --> 00:14:33.830
to be able to understand
the effect of introducing

00:14:33.830 --> 00:14:37.190
a new version of a service and
directing a portion of doing

00:14:37.190 --> 00:14:38.970
canary testing for example.

00:14:38.970 --> 00:14:41.660
So it's a lot of work
to be done there.

00:14:41.660 --> 00:14:44.150
And I mentioned this
before around the X

00:14:44.150 --> 00:14:48.500
as a service, whatever it is
as a service, adoption of those

00:14:48.500 --> 00:14:53.910
in order to accelerate the
actual application development

00:14:53.910 --> 00:14:54.410
process.

00:14:58.160 --> 00:15:00.040
So the cloud challenge.

00:15:00.040 --> 00:15:02.570
We are transforming
far more than IT.

00:15:02.570 --> 00:15:05.220
And we are barely
skirting around the edges

00:15:05.220 --> 00:15:08.600
of the non-IT elements in
this particular presentation,

00:15:08.600 --> 00:15:10.810
but there are cultural
shifts that need to happen.

00:15:10.810 --> 00:15:11.890
There are business
processes that

00:15:11.890 --> 00:15:13.540
need to happen, the
security processes,

00:15:13.540 --> 00:15:16.510
the legislative stuff that
we've got to deal with.

00:15:16.510 --> 00:15:19.930
When we look at our typical
customer scenarios of what they

00:15:19.930 --> 00:15:24.130
go through when they start
looking at transformation

00:15:24.130 --> 00:15:27.700
and moving to public cloud,
quite often the starting point

00:15:27.700 --> 00:15:30.650
is looking at their regulatory
and compliance needs.

00:15:30.650 --> 00:15:33.900
And that typically forms
some sort of a concept

00:15:33.900 --> 00:15:36.960
or architecture around what
they need to do for security,

00:15:36.960 --> 00:15:40.982
and their networking, and
usually is fairly traditional

00:15:40.982 --> 00:15:42.690
based rather than
cloud native approaches

00:15:42.690 --> 00:15:45.265
that are adopted because,
at that stage, all of us

00:15:45.265 --> 00:15:46.140
are in the same boat.

00:15:46.140 --> 00:15:49.810
We haven't quite learned
what we need to be doing.

00:15:49.810 --> 00:15:52.620
Then we start looking at the
selective applications being

00:15:52.620 --> 00:15:56.400
transformed or adopting
ready-made cloud native

00:15:56.400 --> 00:15:58.840
applications in order
to satisfy our needs.

00:15:58.840 --> 00:16:00.480
So we then end up
in a position where

00:16:00.480 --> 00:16:02.970
we've got selective applications
are on public cloud.

00:16:02.970 --> 00:16:05.430
The rest of our
environment is still

00:16:05.430 --> 00:16:08.590
in a non-cloud native
mode and not on-prem.

00:16:08.590 --> 00:16:11.940
And it is usually at this
stage that most businesses

00:16:11.940 --> 00:16:14.160
that we have been
working with have

00:16:14.160 --> 00:16:17.460
a realization that the pace
of public cloud adoption

00:16:17.460 --> 00:16:19.830
is far, far slower
than they would like.

00:16:19.830 --> 00:16:22.383
And in order to accelerate
that, most organizations

00:16:22.383 --> 00:16:24.300
go down the path of
looking to lift and shift.

00:16:27.120 --> 00:16:28.980
There's a few problems.

00:16:28.980 --> 00:16:33.470
So we've got our existing
application space, the legacy

00:16:33.470 --> 00:16:35.630
applications or traditional
application space which

00:16:35.630 --> 00:16:39.080
is not cloud native, traditional
operations around it,

00:16:39.080 --> 00:16:41.780
traditional security around it.

00:16:41.780 --> 00:16:45.440
You're talking about change
control boards, cabs,

00:16:45.440 --> 00:16:49.290
people approving changes
being applied into production.

00:16:49.290 --> 00:16:51.048
And now we've got a
set of applications

00:16:51.048 --> 00:16:53.090
which are completely
different in the way they're

00:16:53.090 --> 00:16:56.120
being managed and they're
cloud native and living

00:16:56.120 --> 00:16:57.210
in public cloud.

00:16:57.210 --> 00:16:58.700
So one or two things can happen.

00:16:58.700 --> 00:17:02.090
You either enforce your
existing IT processes

00:17:02.090 --> 00:17:03.530
on top of your
cloud native stuff

00:17:03.530 --> 00:17:06.319
that have been deployed on
public cloud, in which case

00:17:06.319 --> 00:17:09.950
you are reducing the agility
of the entire solution,

00:17:09.950 --> 00:17:12.770
or you end up in a
mixed mode of operations

00:17:12.770 --> 00:17:14.540
where the cloud
native stuff is dealt

00:17:14.540 --> 00:17:16.819
with in a different
way to your on-prem

00:17:16.819 --> 00:17:19.640
or traditional applications.

00:17:19.640 --> 00:17:22.130
And if it's not planned
upfront really well,

00:17:22.130 --> 00:17:25.440
you end up with an inconsistent
application of controls.

00:17:25.440 --> 00:17:28.020
So governance, IT governance,
technical governance,

00:17:28.020 --> 00:17:31.070
security governance-- it
can end up in a position

00:17:31.070 --> 00:17:35.960
where you have a complete
mismatch and inability

00:17:35.960 --> 00:17:39.590
to consciously say that
you've applied your governance

00:17:39.590 --> 00:17:41.180
practices equally
across the board.

00:17:45.270 --> 00:17:48.770
So when we start looking
at a holistic hybrid cloud,

00:17:48.770 --> 00:17:51.240
and I intentionally keep
putting the word holistic here

00:17:51.240 --> 00:17:53.640
because it's much more than
just the cloud platforms

00:17:53.640 --> 00:17:55.440
themselves-- when
we start looking

00:17:55.440 --> 00:17:59.165
at a holistic hybrid cloud
ecosystem, what does it do?

00:17:59.165 --> 00:18:02.280
It extends and brings the
cloud to your premises.

00:18:02.280 --> 00:18:03.900
Simplistic.

00:18:03.900 --> 00:18:07.290
Allows you to do a coexistent
model and a bimodal mode

00:18:07.290 --> 00:18:08.050
of operations.

00:18:08.050 --> 00:18:11.260
So we have to delve
into that much more.

00:18:11.260 --> 00:18:13.470
But there are certain
characteristics

00:18:13.470 --> 00:18:18.020
that these clouds need to
have in order to enable that.

00:18:18.020 --> 00:18:20.390
But they also enable us,
at least in the short term,

00:18:20.390 --> 00:18:22.170
to protect our
existing investments.

00:18:22.170 --> 00:18:24.560
And we have a hell
of a lot of customers

00:18:24.560 --> 00:18:27.710
who are going through this
journey to public cloud.

00:18:27.710 --> 00:18:30.300
And the reality of the situation
is, and this is not uncommon.

00:18:30.300 --> 00:18:33.400
We talk to them and they've
got a 10-year lease on a data

00:18:33.400 --> 00:18:38.300
center that cannot
be changed overnight.

00:18:38.300 --> 00:18:41.140
They've got that existing
investment and finding a way

00:18:41.140 --> 00:18:44.150
where we are protecting
that existing investment is

00:18:44.150 --> 00:18:44.730
critical.

00:18:44.730 --> 00:18:46.700
And I think hybrid clouds
is one way to address

00:18:46.700 --> 00:18:49.650
that in the short term.

00:18:49.650 --> 00:18:52.580
So what are the characteristics
of a hybrid cloud in order

00:18:52.580 --> 00:18:54.950
to enable those things?

00:18:54.950 --> 00:18:57.860
These are
[? opinionated. ?] Need

00:18:57.860 --> 00:18:59.930
to be open standards
and native API based.

00:18:59.930 --> 00:19:05.110
By that I mean, for example,
open source, standard APIs

00:19:05.110 --> 00:19:07.870
that are not proprietary.

00:19:07.870 --> 00:19:11.850
And that is to enable
essentially integration

00:19:11.850 --> 00:19:15.930
with orchestration tools
with CMP tools that

00:19:15.930 --> 00:19:20.090
are available out there
without getting locked in.

00:19:22.700 --> 00:19:25.310
Need true hybrid
capability in order

00:19:25.310 --> 00:19:27.840
such that if you start
developing your applications

00:19:27.840 --> 00:19:31.380
on the private cloud
element of a hybrid,

00:19:31.380 --> 00:19:36.170
you don't have to spend the same
R&amp;D or application development

00:19:36.170 --> 00:19:38.760
time to then transform
and run in public.

00:19:38.760 --> 00:19:40.700
You want to do the
development work once.

00:19:40.700 --> 00:19:42.850
You do it once.

00:19:42.850 --> 00:19:45.017
In a true hybrid
cloud environment,

00:19:45.017 --> 00:19:47.350
you should be able to have
the full portability in order

00:19:47.350 --> 00:19:49.030
to move them to
public cloud when

00:19:49.030 --> 00:19:50.980
the rest of the processes
and things that you

00:19:50.980 --> 00:19:54.790
need to change within the
organization are ready for it.

00:19:54.790 --> 00:19:56.830
CI/CD and policy driven
architecture we've

00:19:56.830 --> 00:19:58.240
talked about already.

00:19:58.240 --> 00:20:00.940
Absolutely crucial that
your hybrid cloud solutions

00:20:00.940 --> 00:20:03.340
are able to support
these methodologies.

00:20:03.340 --> 00:20:05.230
And again, it goes
back to the point

00:20:05.230 --> 00:20:11.290
of being able to present out
standardized non-proprietary

00:20:11.290 --> 00:20:12.070
open APIs.

00:20:12.070 --> 00:20:13.710
That means that
whatever work you

00:20:13.710 --> 00:20:16.835
do in developing your CI/CD
pipelines, again, is reusable

00:20:16.835 --> 00:20:18.460
regardless of what
cloud you run it on.

00:20:21.440 --> 00:20:22.830
And this is another key point.

00:20:22.830 --> 00:20:27.270
So we still have got our
existing traditional IT service

00:20:27.270 --> 00:20:27.940
processes.

00:20:27.940 --> 00:20:30.330
They're not going to
go away overnight.

00:20:30.330 --> 00:20:33.990
And we need to maintain those
services and processes in order

00:20:33.990 --> 00:20:36.250
to ensure our governance
is still there.

00:20:36.250 --> 00:20:39.600
So we still need a way of
bridging the old and the new.

00:20:39.600 --> 00:20:42.630
So a true hybrid
cloud solution needs

00:20:42.630 --> 00:20:46.020
to give you the capability
whereby you're integrating

00:20:46.020 --> 00:20:51.980
your actual processes, your
ITSM toolsets with that platform

00:20:51.980 --> 00:20:54.520
but in a way where it's
not affecting agility

00:20:54.520 --> 00:20:57.310
and the ability to use
CI/CD capabilities.

00:20:57.310 --> 00:20:58.810
It's a hard thing
to do, but I think

00:20:58.810 --> 00:21:01.390
that is an absolute
crucial requirement

00:21:01.390 --> 00:21:03.080
for a true hybrid
cloud solution.

00:21:06.690 --> 00:21:09.810
Many of you know
adoption of public cloud.

00:21:09.810 --> 00:21:16.920
Again, it's a topic that is
driven sometimes by emotion.

00:21:16.920 --> 00:21:21.570
To be truly successful
in adopting cloud,

00:21:21.570 --> 00:21:24.960
your developers need to love
working in that environment.

00:21:24.960 --> 00:21:26.400
They need to see advantage.

00:21:26.400 --> 00:21:29.200
They need to see a
different way of working.

00:21:29.200 --> 00:21:30.360
They'll have to like it.

00:21:30.360 --> 00:21:32.890
If they don't like it, they
will go and do something else.

00:21:32.890 --> 00:21:34.090
That's the reality.

00:21:34.090 --> 00:21:37.950
We've seen adoption of public
cloud come about in pockets

00:21:37.950 --> 00:21:40.380
of line of business
applications within our clients

00:21:40.380 --> 00:21:43.080
purely because the developers
like the ecosystem that they

00:21:43.080 --> 00:21:44.710
have on public cloud.

00:21:44.710 --> 00:21:49.050
So to make hybrid
cloud successful,

00:21:49.050 --> 00:21:51.293
we need to make sure we are
providing the developers

00:21:51.293 --> 00:21:52.710
with the same set
of capabilities,

00:21:52.710 --> 00:21:55.800
the same set of tools that
they actually want to use,

00:21:55.800 --> 00:21:58.920
and helps them to develop
their applications on-prem.

00:22:02.280 --> 00:22:04.070
So why is it
different this time?

00:22:04.070 --> 00:22:07.070
There's been so many iterations
of technologies changing,

00:22:07.070 --> 00:22:08.700
new things coming along.

00:22:08.700 --> 00:22:10.460
We had virtualization
10 years back

00:22:10.460 --> 00:22:12.260
coming along and
with the promises

00:22:12.260 --> 00:22:14.090
that it would
completely transform

00:22:14.090 --> 00:22:16.465
the way we do business.

00:22:16.465 --> 00:22:18.590
I do think we're at the
unique point at the moment.

00:22:18.590 --> 00:22:23.390
We've got a real convergence of
the infrastructure, application

00:22:23.390 --> 00:22:26.222
and security, technologies,
methodologies,

00:22:26.222 --> 00:22:27.680
all of those things
coming together

00:22:27.680 --> 00:22:32.780
so DevSecOps, CI/CD
capabilities, automated QA,

00:22:32.780 --> 00:22:37.160
cloud native stateless
applications, AI/ML, analytics.

00:22:37.160 --> 00:22:41.660
All of these things have come
to converge at the same time

00:22:41.660 --> 00:22:45.138
to enable us to do a far more
successful transformation

00:22:45.138 --> 00:22:46.430
than we were able to do before.

00:22:49.100 --> 00:22:51.060
And the hybrid cloud
environment has

00:22:51.060 --> 00:22:53.670
got to enable you
to take advantage

00:22:53.670 --> 00:22:55.320
of all of those
technologies and has

00:22:55.320 --> 00:22:58.025
to actually drive the usage
of those technologies.

00:23:00.940 --> 00:23:04.060
So we've only barely
touched on these things.

00:23:04.060 --> 00:23:06.417
But there's a hell of a
lot of new technologies.

00:23:06.417 --> 00:23:09.000
There's a hell of a lot of new
processes, new ways of thinking

00:23:09.000 --> 00:23:10.380
about things.

00:23:10.380 --> 00:23:14.120
There's a lot of
learning to be done

00:23:14.120 --> 00:23:18.252
by the entirety of
our organizations.

00:23:18.252 --> 00:23:20.670
And it's challenging.

00:23:20.670 --> 00:23:22.580
It's really crucial,
I think, for everyone

00:23:22.580 --> 00:23:26.270
to think about working
with trusted partners that

00:23:26.270 --> 00:23:30.450
can help them work through
some of these challenges,

00:23:30.450 --> 00:23:32.360
bring the right
skills to the table,

00:23:32.360 --> 00:23:36.060
and help organizations adapt
their existing application

00:23:36.060 --> 00:23:40.280
development practices
to be DevSecOps based,

00:23:40.280 --> 00:23:43.490
teach you about the technologies
where it's necessary,

00:23:43.490 --> 00:23:44.850
and adopt all of these things.

00:23:44.850 --> 00:23:47.390
I think it's crucial
to try to avoid

00:23:47.390 --> 00:23:53.660
all of the typical pitfalls by
getting help from your trusted

00:23:53.660 --> 00:23:54.920
partners.

00:23:54.920 --> 00:23:59.180
In our case, when we have a
partnership between someone

00:23:59.180 --> 00:24:02.000
that is helping you transform
your business and the cloud

00:24:02.000 --> 00:24:04.250
provider, it's a
three way partnership,

00:24:04.250 --> 00:24:05.720
which brings a lot more value.

00:24:08.778 --> 00:24:10.570
So I'm going to quickly
summarize the stuff

00:24:10.570 --> 00:24:13.150
that we talked about today.

00:24:13.150 --> 00:24:15.530
Transformation is
at every level.

00:24:15.530 --> 00:24:17.440
It's not just in the
technology space.

00:24:17.440 --> 00:24:18.850
It is in the people space.

00:24:18.850 --> 00:24:21.140
It is in the culture
of the organization.

00:24:21.140 --> 00:24:23.930
It is the entirety of
your business processes,

00:24:23.930 --> 00:24:26.170
your security processes,
your application development

00:24:26.170 --> 00:24:26.780
processes.

00:24:26.780 --> 00:24:29.020
So it is at every single level.

00:24:29.020 --> 00:24:30.820
But this is the price
of getting agility.

00:24:30.820 --> 00:24:33.310
If you want your businesses
to be agile, and be

00:24:33.310 --> 00:24:35.980
able to compete, and come
up with those differentiated

00:24:35.980 --> 00:24:38.380
offerings, this is the
sort of transformation

00:24:38.380 --> 00:24:41.068
that needs to be taking place.

00:24:41.068 --> 00:24:42.610
We need to get to
a place where we're

00:24:42.610 --> 00:24:46.540
able to encode business
processes, our governance,

00:24:46.540 --> 00:24:51.930
our security all into automated
ways of applying those,

00:24:51.930 --> 00:24:57.730
so CI/CD through automated
policy enforcement.

00:24:57.730 --> 00:25:00.640
We have to ensure that whatever
solutions we put forward

00:25:00.640 --> 00:25:03.670
are capable of
supporting these things.

00:25:03.670 --> 00:25:06.760
Hybrid clouds can significantly
accelerate movement

00:25:06.760 --> 00:25:10.340
to cloud, public cloud, cloud
overall, adoption of cloud.

00:25:10.340 --> 00:25:10.840
Why?

00:25:10.840 --> 00:25:14.180
Because they allow you to
parallelize your activity.

00:25:14.180 --> 00:25:16.330
So if you looked at
that typical scenario

00:25:16.330 --> 00:25:21.100
that I was talking through
earlier where we were talking

00:25:21.100 --> 00:25:24.850
to the legislators
or governing bodies,

00:25:24.850 --> 00:25:26.890
see what we're
allowed to do, looking

00:25:26.890 --> 00:25:28.540
at changing our
security practices,

00:25:28.540 --> 00:25:30.460
looking at changing
our network practices.

00:25:30.460 --> 00:25:34.180
Once we've done that, we start
doing pockets of applications.

00:25:34.180 --> 00:25:35.890
If you have a hybrid
cloud environment

00:25:35.890 --> 00:25:37.900
to do your development
work on, and it's

00:25:37.900 --> 00:25:40.330
got all of the same capabilities
or the vast majority

00:25:40.330 --> 00:25:43.030
of the core capabilities that
you need for your developers

00:25:43.030 --> 00:25:45.460
to do cloud native
application development

00:25:45.460 --> 00:25:48.280
but on-prem within the
confines of your own security

00:25:48.280 --> 00:25:50.900
space, your existing
network space, et cetera,

00:25:50.900 --> 00:25:53.998
you're [? parallelizing ?]
that set of activities.

00:25:53.998 --> 00:25:55.540
So by nature, you're
going to shorten

00:25:55.540 --> 00:25:59.670
the time scales required to
do the overall transformation.

00:25:59.670 --> 00:26:02.900
And it's leading by example.

00:26:02.900 --> 00:26:06.490
When it comes to bringing the
legislators or auditing bodies

00:26:06.490 --> 00:26:09.100
alongside with you, you
have real life examples

00:26:09.100 --> 00:26:13.360
running on-prem that are
compliant to the existing

00:26:13.360 --> 00:26:15.970
security requirements but also
demonstrate what can be done.

00:26:19.935 --> 00:26:21.310
We talked about
the hybrid clouds

00:26:21.310 --> 00:26:23.560
needing to provide the right
ecosystem that developers

00:26:23.560 --> 00:26:25.345
want to adopt.

00:26:25.345 --> 00:26:26.845
They've got to be
developer centric.

00:26:30.190 --> 00:26:32.643
If we don't hit that
piece, like I said,

00:26:32.643 --> 00:26:34.060
your developers
will go elsewhere,

00:26:34.060 --> 00:26:35.130
do it some other way.

00:26:35.130 --> 00:26:37.350
And we end up with that
mixed governance model

00:26:37.350 --> 00:26:40.510
and the inability to apply
the right level of governance

00:26:40.510 --> 00:26:45.610
controls where all
of us, including me,

00:26:45.610 --> 00:26:48.543
on a learning journey
continuously, some

00:26:48.543 --> 00:26:50.210
of these technologies
are bleeding edge.

00:26:50.210 --> 00:26:51.430
They're developing.

00:26:51.430 --> 00:26:54.790
Every day something new happens,
something new functionality

00:26:54.790 --> 00:26:56.410
comes out.

00:26:56.410 --> 00:26:58.420
So it's working with
your trusted partners

00:26:58.420 --> 00:27:01.411
in order to accelerate and
de-risk that transformation.

00:27:04.240 --> 00:27:07.530
So I'm really proud and excited
to present the Atos OpenHybrid

00:27:07.530 --> 00:27:10.590
Cloud platform, which was
launched on Tuesday morning

00:27:10.590 --> 00:27:12.768
at this event.

00:27:12.768 --> 00:27:15.060
I'm going to try to keep the
marketing elements of this

00:27:15.060 --> 00:27:17.370
to a bare minimum.

00:27:17.370 --> 00:27:21.630
But in short, OHC is a
managed hybrid cloud platform

00:27:21.630 --> 00:27:25.830
and that at a base
level provides both IaaS

00:27:25.830 --> 00:27:27.960
and Kubernetes in
the same box, which

00:27:27.960 --> 00:27:30.900
means you have dynamic
scaling between the two.

00:27:30.900 --> 00:27:32.700
It provides SaaS and
PaaS services that

00:27:32.700 --> 00:27:36.260
are mirroring what is on GCP--

00:27:36.260 --> 00:27:40.342
database as a service, big
data as a service, AI/ML.

00:27:40.342 --> 00:27:42.150
And it's got hybrid
capabilities.

00:27:42.150 --> 00:27:44.910
So I'm sure you've seen the
keynote speeches on Anthos.

00:27:44.910 --> 00:27:48.010
Anthos is embedded
within this product.

00:27:48.010 --> 00:27:49.950
And you are able
to use this product

00:27:49.950 --> 00:27:53.560
in both a true private mode--

00:27:53.560 --> 00:27:58.080
so if you're not quite ready
to open up your network

00:27:58.080 --> 00:27:59.640
and have a true
hybrid capability,

00:27:59.640 --> 00:28:02.340
you can start off
with it being private

00:28:02.340 --> 00:28:04.320
and dynamically
change configuration

00:28:04.320 --> 00:28:05.500
to move it into hybrid mode.

00:28:09.430 --> 00:28:12.130
OHC is cost optimized.

00:28:12.130 --> 00:28:15.010
It is entirely based on
open source technologies.

00:28:15.010 --> 00:28:17.410
And we have been working
with our partners

00:28:17.410 --> 00:28:21.010
Google to ensure the
set of technologies

00:28:21.010 --> 00:28:23.780
that we built on this
platform are API compatible.

00:28:23.780 --> 00:28:25.403
And again, going
back to the point

00:28:25.403 --> 00:28:27.820
of being able to develop your
applications once and deploy

00:28:27.820 --> 00:28:30.253
them into any cloud,
that's the work

00:28:30.253 --> 00:28:32.170
that we've been doing
for the past four months

00:28:32.170 --> 00:28:36.270
in order to ensure
that interoperability.

00:28:36.270 --> 00:28:39.730
From a DevOps
perspective, all the APIs

00:28:39.730 --> 00:28:40.980
are available for consumption.

00:28:40.980 --> 00:28:44.430
The native APIs-- there
are no proprietary APIs.

00:28:44.430 --> 00:28:47.880
Everything that is on the
platform is open standards.

00:28:47.880 --> 00:28:51.110
And all the third party tools
for orchestration, CMPs, et

00:28:51.110 --> 00:28:54.810
cetera, all integrate
with it out of the box.

00:28:54.810 --> 00:28:56.640
It is really
intended as a gateway

00:28:56.640 --> 00:28:59.610
to accelerate move
to public cloud.

00:28:59.610 --> 00:29:01.980
And it is extremely
developer centric.

00:29:01.980 --> 00:29:04.550
So like I said, all of
the APIs are fully exposed

00:29:04.550 --> 00:29:06.020
and they are the native APIs.

00:29:09.180 --> 00:29:12.450
We are the first Google
partner to implement an ethos

00:29:12.450 --> 00:29:15.150
on top of OpenStack.

00:29:15.150 --> 00:29:16.900
There's a lot of
advantages on that front,

00:29:16.900 --> 00:29:19.350
including the cost
side of things.

00:29:19.350 --> 00:29:22.290
But it does allow us to
offer a platform which

00:29:22.290 --> 00:29:26.790
is a combination of the IaaS
and the CaaS side of things.

00:29:26.790 --> 00:29:29.790
But more crucially,
the ecosystem

00:29:29.790 --> 00:29:33.900
that you see on the screen, it
isn't just the hosting elements

00:29:33.900 --> 00:29:35.670
of a cloud platform.

00:29:35.670 --> 00:29:37.530
It is all the data
services, the key data

00:29:37.530 --> 00:29:40.560
services that any application
developer developing

00:29:40.560 --> 00:29:42.990
microservices would want to use.

00:29:42.990 --> 00:29:46.220
And above and beyond that,
your capabilities are on AI/ML.

00:29:46.220 --> 00:29:50.760
There's variants of this in
terms of the hardware models

00:29:50.760 --> 00:29:53.010
where we've got
very [? high end ?]

00:29:53.010 --> 00:29:54.250
GPU acceleration.

00:29:54.250 --> 00:29:57.298
So again, you can do even
the Kubeflow and AI/ML

00:29:57.298 --> 00:29:58.590
aspects of it on this platform.

00:30:01.960 --> 00:30:06.610
The solution itself incorporates
the concept of policy engine.

00:30:06.610 --> 00:30:08.810
Policy engine, and let
me be clear about this,

00:30:08.810 --> 00:30:11.800
because there's a lot of
different uses of the word

00:30:11.800 --> 00:30:12.580
policy engine.

00:30:12.580 --> 00:30:16.180
Many people think of it in terms
of identity access management.

00:30:16.180 --> 00:30:17.720
That's not what
I'm talking about.

00:30:17.720 --> 00:30:20.980
I'm talking about policies
that represent genuine business

00:30:20.980 --> 00:30:22.330
level policies.

00:30:22.330 --> 00:30:24.460
So take an example.

00:30:24.460 --> 00:30:26.050
An application has
got a requirement

00:30:26.050 --> 00:30:28.660
to be PCI DSS compliance.

00:30:28.660 --> 00:30:30.550
So that application
can be tagged

00:30:30.550 --> 00:30:32.890
to be PCI DSS that's compliant.

00:30:32.890 --> 00:30:35.440
Your pipelines and
your policy engine

00:30:35.440 --> 00:30:36.970
must prevent that
application being

00:30:36.970 --> 00:30:41.480
deployed on top of a platform
which is not PCI DSS compliant.

00:30:41.480 --> 00:30:42.970
And when it gets
down to the level

00:30:42.970 --> 00:30:45.910
of the actual
technical components

00:30:45.910 --> 00:30:47.800
and the way the
services are delivered

00:30:47.800 --> 00:30:49.600
and the characteristics
of that platform,

00:30:49.600 --> 00:30:51.910
that's where the policy
enforcement comes in and is

00:30:51.910 --> 00:30:55.210
able to discern whether this
platform is suitable to deploy

00:30:55.210 --> 00:30:57.077
such an application on top of.

00:30:57.077 --> 00:30:58.660
It could be more
simplistic than that.

00:30:58.660 --> 00:30:59.890
Geofencing.

00:30:59.890 --> 00:31:02.170
So we know many countries
around the world

00:31:02.170 --> 00:31:04.390
have got policies
about certain data

00:31:04.390 --> 00:31:06.220
not leaving their perimeters.

00:31:06.220 --> 00:31:09.160
We're able to, for example,
apply a geofencing policy

00:31:09.160 --> 00:31:11.920
that says the application
can be deployed

00:31:11.920 --> 00:31:14.830
in this particular instance
of the private cloud

00:31:14.830 --> 00:31:19.220
or in that region of a
public cloud, but not others.

00:31:19.220 --> 00:31:23.680
So we're talking about the
high level business level

00:31:23.680 --> 00:31:26.560
policies which then
our solution translates

00:31:26.560 --> 00:31:30.595
into a much lower level granular
technical implementation

00:31:30.595 --> 00:31:31.345
of those policies.

00:31:37.930 --> 00:31:41.410
So there's a couple of more
elements I want to touch on.

00:31:41.410 --> 00:31:44.580
So we talked earlier
on about hybrid cloud

00:31:44.580 --> 00:31:49.660
integrating with existing ITIL
processes, ITSM tool sets.

00:31:49.660 --> 00:31:52.500
So we have created a
level of abstraction

00:31:52.500 --> 00:31:54.480
specifically for that purpose.

00:31:54.480 --> 00:31:57.360
So we have message
buses, which are actually

00:31:57.360 --> 00:32:01.800
based on Google
Pub/Sub, where we

00:32:01.800 --> 00:32:03.930
are able to publish
all of the messages

00:32:03.930 --> 00:32:07.700
from an event alerting
and CMTB perspective.

00:32:07.700 --> 00:32:11.220
So you create a new instance
of a virtual machine,

00:32:11.220 --> 00:32:13.540
or you create a new
Kubernetes cluster,

00:32:13.540 --> 00:32:15.865
or you spin up a new
application on Kubernetes.

00:32:15.865 --> 00:32:17.490
Events will be
published so they can be

00:32:17.490 --> 00:32:18.990
consumed by existing tool sets.

00:32:18.990 --> 00:32:22.110
You can still apply your
auditing and compliance

00:32:22.110 --> 00:32:24.150
using your existing tool sets.

00:32:24.150 --> 00:32:26.190
The solution itself
has not got a reliance

00:32:26.190 --> 00:32:29.610
to manage services around
the solution or DevOps mode

00:32:29.610 --> 00:32:31.800
and don't have a reliance
on those ITSM tools.

00:32:31.800 --> 00:32:35.910
But it does allow
that bimodal operation

00:32:35.910 --> 00:32:39.630
and marrying up the existing
ITIL processes with the DevOps

00:32:39.630 --> 00:32:41.610
side of things.

00:32:41.610 --> 00:32:44.017
So talking about that
message bus and the Pub/Sub,

00:32:44.017 --> 00:32:45.600
we have an implementation
that pops up

00:32:45.600 --> 00:32:47.400
within this platform itself.

00:32:47.400 --> 00:32:51.360
We worked with Google to
actually create a solution

00:32:51.360 --> 00:32:56.370
where whereby we've got 100% API
compatible version of Pub/Sub

00:32:56.370 --> 00:32:58.470
running [? free ?] locally.

00:32:58.470 --> 00:33:02.760
And in hybrid mode, it
creates a connection to GCP

00:33:02.760 --> 00:33:05.550
and gives you a single
message bus across GCP

00:33:05.550 --> 00:33:07.712
and your private cloud.

00:33:07.712 --> 00:33:09.420
It's a really powerful
concept because it

00:33:09.420 --> 00:33:11.587
does mean that events, all
of those types of things,

00:33:11.587 --> 00:33:14.220
again can be correlated
and collected

00:33:14.220 --> 00:33:18.990
in a multi-cloud or hybrid-cloud
scenario-- centralized, viewed,

00:33:18.990 --> 00:33:20.202
analyzed.

00:33:20.202 --> 00:33:21.660
All of those things
become possible

00:33:21.660 --> 00:33:25.290
when you've got ability to span
with one set of technologies

00:33:25.290 --> 00:33:27.750
message buses across
one or more clouds.

00:33:32.330 --> 00:33:33.560
Manage databases.

00:33:33.560 --> 00:33:35.570
Manage the big data services.

00:33:35.570 --> 00:33:37.010
Again, these are accelerators.

00:33:37.010 --> 00:33:40.040
And because they're API
compatible with the Google

00:33:40.040 --> 00:33:42.020
services, it's the same concept.

00:33:42.020 --> 00:33:45.260
You write the application
once and you're

00:33:45.260 --> 00:33:49.150
able to deploy and consume it
in multiple clouds, private,

00:33:49.150 --> 00:33:51.620
or on premise, or on public.

00:33:59.010 --> 00:34:03.920
We've got demos of our
platform in the stall.

00:34:03.920 --> 00:34:06.540
And I would really welcome
you to come and join us.

00:34:06.540 --> 00:34:07.730
So these are live demos.

00:34:07.730 --> 00:34:09.847
They're not prerecorded.

00:34:09.847 --> 00:34:11.389
We've got a number
of different ones.

00:34:11.389 --> 00:34:13.222
We've got an overview
where we just show you

00:34:13.222 --> 00:34:15.060
around what the
product looks like

00:34:15.060 --> 00:34:16.940
and what the APIs they
offer is and the user

00:34:16.940 --> 00:34:19.580
interface within it.

00:34:19.580 --> 00:34:22.610
We demonstrate a
hybrid application

00:34:22.610 --> 00:34:25.370
being deployed onto
the private cloud,

00:34:25.370 --> 00:34:29.090
the same hybrid application
being deployed to public cloud,

00:34:29.090 --> 00:34:31.880
and then we split the
application into multiple parts

00:34:31.880 --> 00:34:33.710
so the front end web
services for example

00:34:33.710 --> 00:34:37.310
run on GCP using ASD service
mesh communicating back

00:34:37.310 --> 00:34:40.800
to database services
running on-prem.

00:34:40.800 --> 00:34:42.850
So your data stayed on-prem.

00:34:42.850 --> 00:34:45.620
Your web service is scalable.

00:34:45.620 --> 00:34:48.489
Burst out if you
like into public.

00:34:48.489 --> 00:34:49.889
So it's quite powerful stuff.

00:34:49.889 --> 00:34:51.264
And we have got
a demo of Anthos.

00:34:51.264 --> 00:34:53.610
So we can actually go
into Google console.

00:34:53.610 --> 00:34:55.580
We see the private
cloud as appearing

00:34:55.580 --> 00:34:58.160
as a region within
the GCP console

00:34:58.160 --> 00:34:59.420
and go into the marketplace.

00:34:59.420 --> 00:35:02.360
You can select an
application for marketplace

00:35:02.360 --> 00:35:05.275
and deploy it directly
onto the private cloud.

00:35:05.275 --> 00:35:06.650
It's quite a
powerful thing to be

00:35:06.650 --> 00:35:09.320
able to use the marketplace,
all of the work that

00:35:09.320 --> 00:35:11.540
goes into creating these
templated applications

00:35:11.540 --> 00:35:13.760
and being able to reuse
those regardless of the fact

00:35:13.760 --> 00:35:14.968
that you are running on-prem.

